.TH HOSTNAME 1
.SH NAME
hostname \- print or set the name of this machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hostname
[\ name\ ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hostname
prints the name of this machine. Given a
.IR name ,
it makes that the machine's name; only root can. A name is 1 to 8
letters, digits and \-.
.PP
XENIX keeps the machine's name as the first line of /etc/systemid, the
file Micnet and UUCP read it from, and that is where hostname reads and
writes it. Any lines after the first are left as they are.
.PP
Setting the name also gives it to the kernel, as the node name that
.B uname \-n
and
.B uname \-a
print, by writing it into the kernel's memory through /dev/kmem. The
kernel forgets it when the machine is started again, and
.BR asktime (1)
gives it back at every start.
.PP
The C shell prompt that mkuser gives new accounts shows this name.
.SH FILES
.TP 16
/etc/systemid
the machine's name, on its first line
.TP
/xenix
where in the kernel the node name is
.TP
/dev/kmem
the kernel's memory
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is 0 when the name was printed or set, 1 when
/etc/systemid could not be read or written, the name is not a name, or
the kernel's node name could not be set, and 2 after a mistake in the
arguments.
.SH SEE ALSO
asktime(1), uname(1)
.SH HISTORY
A
.B hostname
command appeared in 4.2BSD.
.SH AUTHORS
This hostname was written for the xenix86-devsys system tools.
.SH BUGS
A name is at most 8 characters, the most the kernel's node name holds.
